      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate markdown carefully.\n\n**Certificate details:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2023.01\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- Page total: 10\n- Date issued: 31 January 2023 (Date: 31 January 2023 on page 2)\n- OIML issuer ID: not explicitly stated... There's \"V 3112\" in the logo area but that's not clear. I'll omit.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- Address: Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Also on page 2: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body (that's the signatory). The \"person_responsible\" field on page 1 says Jan Kalandra. I could add person_title \"Head of Certification Body\" — but that's associated with Klenovský. Hmm. The schema has person_responsible and person_title. Page 1 says \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". Page 2 signature block: \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and maybe not include person_title since it belongs to Klenovský. Actually could include both but the schema is flat. I'll keep person_responsible: Jan Kalandra, and omit title, or note Klenovský? The signing authority is Klenovský. The schema only has one person_responsible. I'll use Jan Kalandra as stated.\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Cixi Cidong Flow Instrument Co., Ltd, No. 148, Longzhen Road, Longshan Industrial Park, 315 311 Ningbo, Zhejiang (China)\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" — the schema says category examples include \"Gas meter\" etc. Water meter (Woltmann). I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: LXLC. Also LXSG-20 mentioned in marking section — \"The water meters types LXSG-20 shall be clearly...\" That seems like a typo in the cert (LXLC vs LXSG-20), but it's mentioned. Hmm. The marking section says \"water meters types LXSG-20\" — probably a copy-paste error in the original cert. Should I include it? The rule says \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". The dial says LXLC-50. I'll include \"LXLC\" and the models. LXSG-20 is mentioned as marking but likely typo; I could include it to be faithful. Hmm, risk of noise. I think I'll include LXLC as the type designation and mention LXSG-20? Let me be careful: The certified type is \"water meter – Woltmann LXLC\". Marking section mentions LXSG-20 (probably an error in the original document). I'll include it in type_designations? The instruction says \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". I'll include both, with LXLC primary. Actually maybe better to just include \"LXLC\" since LXSG-20 appears to be an error. But faithful extraction says include what's mentioned. I'll include LXLC and note... Schema has description field. I'll put description of the meter and mention LXSG-20 appears in marking section? That might be overkill. Let me include type_designations: [\"LXLC\"] and maybe add \"LXSG-20\" too. Hmm. The marking section explicitly says \"The water meters types LXSG-20 shall be clearly and indelibly marked\". I think including it is more faithful. But it could be a genuine OCR/original typo. I'll include it — the rule says all type identifiers mentioned.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The cert covers type LXLC. LXSG-20 is likely a leftover error in the original certificate text. Including it might mislead. But rule 8 says don't fabricate — including a mentioned identifier is not fabrication. I'll include it.\n\n- module_designation: \"-\" meaning Not applicable. The form says \"Designation of the module (if applicable): -\". So module_designation: \"Not applicable\" or null. I'll use \"Not applicable\".\n\n- description: The measuring system description text. I'll write a concise summary.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- OIML R 49, Edition 2013, accuracy_classes: [\"2\"] (accuracy class 2). Canonical id \"R49\".\n\n**Test reports:**\n- Type evaluation report: No. 0511-UL-V037-21 dated 25 January 2023, 31 pages including annex 1. role: type_evaluation_report.\n- Test report No. 6015-PT-P5002-23, 57 pages including annex 1, 2, 3. role: test_report.\n- Documentation file: 0511-UL-V037-21. role: documentation_file.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Addition 0, 31 January 2023, Issuing certificate. revision: \"0\".\n\n**Models:**\nThe cert covers 6 nominal diameters: LXLC-50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200. Each has two variants by Q3/Q1 ratio: 40 or 50 (for the GB dial plate design; for LYG only 40). Also two dial plate designs: GB (Copper Can Calculator, super dry) and LYG (Plastic Calculator, dry).\n\nSo model variants: nominal diameter × Q3/Q1 ratio × dial plate design. The flowrate table shows for each DN two columns (Q3/Q1=40 and 50).\n\nModels:\n- DN50, R40, GB: Q1=1.250, Q2=2.00, Q3=40, Q4=50\n- DN50, R50, GB: Q1=0.800, Q2=1.28, Q3=40, Q4=50\n- DN65, R40, GB: Q1=1.250, Q2=2.00, Q3=40, Q4=50\n- DN65, R50, GB: Q1=0.800, Q2=1.28, Q3=40, Q4=50\n- DN80, R40, GB: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=63, Q4=78.75\n- DN80, R50, GB: Q1=1.260, Q2=2.02, Q3=63, Q4=78.75\n- DN100, R40, GB: Q1=2.500, Q2=4.00, Q3=100, Q4=125\n- DN100, R50, GB: Q1=2.00, Q2=3.20, Q3=100, Q4=125\n- DN150, R40, GB: Q1=6.250, Q2=10.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5\n- DN150, R50, GB: Q1=5.00, Q2=8.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5\n- DN200, R40, GB: Q1=10.00, Q2=16.00, Q3=400, Q4=500\n- DN200, R50, GB: Q1=8.00, Q2=12.8, Q3=400, Q4=500\n\nFor LYG dial plate design: only Q3/Q1 = 40 for all six DN.\n\nNow the question: how to model this. The model_id could be like \"LXLC-50-R40-GB\", etc. Or simpler: \"LXLC-50 (Q3/Q1=40)\". Given the certificate pages:\n\nPage 3-4: GB dial plate, models LXLC-50, 65, 80. Temperature class: T30;T50 and T90 columns (per Q3/Q1 40/50? Actually the temperature class row shows 6 columns: T30;T50, T90, T30;T50, T90, T30;T50, T90 — so for each model there are two sub-columns: R40 → T30;T50, R50 → T90). Interesting: T30/T50 for Q3/Q1=40 and T90 for Q3/Q1=50. Wait, but maximum admissible temperature says \"50 or 90\". Hmm, T30 class means 0-30°C, T50 means 0-50°C, T90 means 0-90°C. So the R40 variants are T30 or T50, and R50 variants are T90.\n\nPage 5: models LXLC-100, 150, 200 with same temp class pattern (T30;T50 / T90 alternating).\n\nPage 5 table shows Indicating range for LXLC-100: 9 999 999, LXLC-150: 99 999 999, LXLC-200: 99 999 999. Resolution: 0.002, 0.02, 0.02. Wait — page 5 table is the continuation: \"Electromagnetic environment\", \"Maximum admissible temperature\", etc. with 3 columns. The indicating range: 9 999 999 (for LXLC-100), 99 999 999 (LXLC-150), 99 999 999 (LXLC-200). Resolution: 0.002, 0.02, 0.02. These are 3 columns for the 3 models (100, 150, 200) — but wait, on page 3-4 the GB table had 3 models each spanning 2 sub-columns for Q3/Q1. For indicating range, colspan=2 each → so the indicating range applies to the model (not per ratio). For LXLC-50/65/80: all 9 999 999, resolution all 0.002.\n\nPage 6: continuation with installation details for LXLC-100/150/200: min straight inlet 1000, 1250, 1500; outlet 500, 625, 750; length 250, 250, 300.\n\nPage 7: LYG dial plate design table: models LXLC-50 through 200, all Q3/Q1=40, temp class T30; T50, indicating range 999 999 (all), resolution: 0.0005, 0.0005, 0.0005, 0.0005, 0.002, 0.002. Min straight lengths: inlet 500, 650, 800, 1000, 1250, 1500; outlet 250, 325, 400, 500, 625, 750. Length: 200, 200, 225, 250, 250, 300.\n\nPage 8: Basic metrological data (flowrates) table: covers all six DN with two columns each (R40, R50). This is the flowrate table — it doesn't distinguish dial plate design, so presumably applies to both? The GB tables on pages 3-5 reference \"flowrates are shown in Table Basic metrological data (flowrates)\", and the LYG table on page 7 also references the same. The GB design has R40 and R50 variants; LYG only R40. The flowrate table gives both columns.\n\nSo the model matrix:\n\nFor each (DN, ratio) combination — that's 12 combos for GB and 6 for LYG... but the flowrate table itself doesn't distinguish GB/LYG; it just says model LXLC. Hmm.\n\nGiven the cert's GB section: temp class differs per ratio (T30;T50 for R40, T90 for R50). The LYG section: all R40, T30;T50.\n\nApproach: define model_family with models as combinations. Could get complex. Let me think about the cleanest representation:\n\nmodel_id format: \"LXLC-{DN}\" with attributes capturing ratio variants? Or model_id \"LXLC-50-R40\", etc.?\n\nThe certificate model numbers are LXLC-50, LXLC-65, LXLC-80, LXLC-100, LXLC-150, LXLC-200. Within each model number, there are variants by Q3/Q1 ratio (40/50) and dial plate design (GB/LYG).\n\nOption A: models = 6 (per model number), with attributes: dial_plate_design, nominal_diameter, ratios: [40, 50] (GB) or [40] (LYG).\n\nOption B: models = 18 combinations (6 DN × 3 design/ratio combos: GB-R40, GB-R50, LYG-R40).\n\nGiven the temp class varies by ratio, and resolution varies by design, Option B is more precise but heavier. The schema says model_level entries: attribute, values: [{model, value}].\n\nI think Option B is cleaner for the matrix: each model_id like \"LXLC-50-R40-GB\". But is that faithful? The certificate doesn't use such designations explicitly. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, use config_level for the ratio axis: attribute varies by configuration axis \"q3_q1_ratio\". E.g., temperature_class by axis q3_q1_ratio: R40 → \"T30; T50\", R50 → \"T90\".\n\nActually let me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"LXLC\"\n- models: 6 entries, model_id \"LXLC-50\" ... \"LXLC-200\", each with attributes: dial_plate_designs [\"GB\", \"LYG\"]... hmm, attributes as single values.\n\nActually, maybe models per design. Let me use models:\n- \"LXLC-50-GB\", \"LXLC-65-GB\", \"LXLC-80-GB\", \"LXLC-100-GB\", \"LXLC-150-GB\", \"LXLC-200-GB\" (with ratio variants 40 and 50)\n- \"LXLC-50-LYG\", ..., \"LXLC-200-LYG\" (ratio 40 only)\n\nThen model_level:\n- nominal_diameter (mm... actually DN in mm): values per model\n- dial_plate_design: GB or LYG\n- q3_q1_ratio: for GB models it's both 40 and 50 → this is config axis. For LYG: 40.\n\nHmm, the ratio variants within GB models → config_level with axis \"q3_q1_ratio\".\n\ntemperature_class: config_level axis \"q3_q1_ratio\": {condition \"40\", value \"T30; T50\"}, {condition \"50\", value \"T90\"} (for GB). For LYG: type_level \"T30; T50\".\n\nActually the temperature class assignment: In GB tables, temperature class row has 6 cells: T30;T50 (LXLC-50 R40), T90 (LXLC-50 R50), T30;T50 (LXLC-65 R40), T90 (LXLC-65 R50), T30;T50 (LXLC-80 R40), T90 (LXLC-80 R50). So yes, per ratio.\n\nFor LYG: all T30; T50.\n\nFlowrate table: applies to both designs (page 8 table doesn't mention design). Values per (DN, ratio).\n\nLet me now define model_level entries:\n\nModels list (Option: 18 combos or 12?). Let me go with 12 models based on (DN, ratio): \"LXLC-50-R40\", \"LXLC-50-R50\", ..., and add dial plate design as attribute: GB models get design GB; but LYG shares R40 flowrates with GB R40... The flowrate table row \"Model number: LXLC\" spans both designs. So R40 flowrates apply to both GB and LYG.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me simplify: models = (DN × ratio × design) = 6 DN × 2 ratios × 2 designs, minus LYG-R50 (doesn't exist) = 24 - 6 = 18.\n\nThat's a lot but it's precise. Alternatively use config axes for design and ratio, keeping 6 models. The schema says config_level for \"attribute × config-axis matrix\". Dial plate design (GB vs LYG) is a genuine config axis; ratio too.\n\nI think a hybrid: models = 6 (LXLC-50 ... LXLC-200) with attribute nominal_diameter. Then:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: dial_plate_design, axis: \"dial_plate_design\", values: [{condition: \"all models\", value: \"GB\"}, ...] — that's awkward.\n\nAlternative: components? The indicating device could be a component: role \"indicating device\", alternatives \"OR\": Plastic Calculator LYG OR Copper Can Calculator GB.\n\nLet me reconsider. The certificate effectively certifies:\n- 6 model numbers (DN 50–200)\n- 2 dial plate designs (GB super dry, LYG dry) — affects resolution and indicating range\n- Q3/Q1 ratio 40 or 50 (LYG only 40) — affects Q1/Q2, temperature class\n\nI'll model:\n\nmodel_family:\n  family_name: \"LXLC\"\n  models: [\n    {model_id: \"LXLC-50\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: 50, q3_q1_ratios: [40,50]}}, ...\n  ]\n\nHmm, attributes{} shape is free-form. But then model_level matrices would use model_id \"LXLC-50\".\n\nThen config_level entries:\n- attribute \"temperature_class\", axis \"q3_q1_ratio\": [{condition: \"40 (dial plate GB and LYG)\", value: \"T30; T50\"}, {condition: \"50 (dial plate GB)\", value: \"T90\"}]\n\nActually wait — for LYG, temp class is T30;T50 and ratio is only 40. For GB, R40 → T30;T50, R50 → T90. So the mapping ratio→temp class holds universally: R40 → T30/T50, R50 → T90.\n\n- attribute \"maximum_admissible_temperature\", axis \"temperature_class\": [{condition \"T30\", value 30}, {condition \"T50\", value 50}, {condition \"T90\", value 90}] unit °C. The cert says \"50 or 90\" (probably ignoring T30 since T50 covers). I'll record as stated: \"50 or 90\" with config on temp class: T30→30? Hmm, max admissible temp for T30 is 30°C by definition, but cert lists \"50 or 90\". I'll keep type_level value \"50 or 90\" as string? That's a list-ish. Better: config_level with axis temperature_class: T30→30, T50→50, T90→90. But that's inference (T30 → 30°C is by definition of class). The cert explicitly lists \"Maximum admissible temperature [°C]: 50 or 90\" for all. To avoid fabrication, I'll record type_level: maximum_admissible_temperature = {value: \"50 or 90\"} as string. Hmm, rule says numbers not strings where possible, but \"50 or 90\" is a list of possible values. Could do config_level by temp class. I think safest: value as string \"50 or 90\" — it's a set of permissible values, not a range. Alternatively record per temp class config: T30/T50 → 50, T90 → 90. That is a faithful reading: for T30/T50 variants max admissible temp is 50, for T90 it's 90. Actually yes — a T30 meter's MAT would be 30°C, but the cert says 50 or 90, matching T50 and T90 variants. I'll use config_level: axis \"temperature_class\", T30/T50 → 50, T90 → 90. Hmm, but T30 → 50? The T30 marking allows 0–30°C. The \"Maximum admissible temperature\" being 50 for T30 variant seems odd but it's what the cert says as a blanket. I'll do the safer route: type_level value \"50 or 90\" as string with unit °C. Actually rules say strings allowed when not numeric. \"50 or 90\" isn't a single number; string is acceptable. Let me do config_level instead — it's cleaner and matches how cert data is used. Eh. I'll go with config_level:\n  {attribute: \"maximum_admissible_temperature\", axis: \"temperature_class\", values: [{condition: \"T30 or T50\", value: 50, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}, {condition: \"T90\", value: 90, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}]}\n\nHmm, that's interpretation. The cert literally says \"50 or 90\" with temperature classes T30;T50 / T90. I think this mapping is safe and correct. But to be strictly non-fabricating, maybe simplest is string \"50 or 90\". I'll go with the config-level mapping because it preserves both facts faithfully and is more useful. Actually, let me reconsider once more — risk assessment: If I put string \"50 or 90\", no information lost. If I put config mapping, slight interpretation. I'll put config mapping; it's clearly derivable and not fabrication.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me reconsider: is the resolution difference GB vs LYG? Page 3-4 (GB, LXLC-50/65/80): resolution 0.002 for all three; indicating range 9 999 999. Page 6 (GB, LXLC-100/150/200): resolution 0.002 (100), 0.02 (150), 0.02 (200); indicating range 9 999 999 (100), 99 999 999 (150), 99 999 999 (200). Page 7 (LYG, all): indicating range 999 999 (all six); resolution 0.0005 (50/65/80/100), 0.002 (150/200).\n\nSo resolution and indicating range vary by design AND DN:\n- GB: 50→0.002, 65→0.002, 80→0.002, 100→0.002, 150→0.02, 200→0.02; range 9 999 999 / 9 999 999 / 9 999 999 / 9 999 999 / 99 999 999 / 99 999 999.\n- LYG: 50–100→0.0005, 150–200→0.002; range 999 999 all.\n\nMin straight lengths and length vary only by DN (same for both designs: inlet 500/650/800/1000/1250/1500; outlet 250/325/400/500/625/750; length 200/200/225/250/250/300). Yes — GB page 4 for 50/65/80: inlet 500/650/800, outlet 250/325/400, length 200/200/225. GB page 6 for 100/150/200: 1000/1250/1500, 500/625/750, 250/250/300. LYG page 7: same values. Good — DN-level attributes.\n\nFlowrates vary by DN × ratio:\n- DN50: R40: Q1=1.250, Q2=2.00, Q3=40, Q4=50; R50: Q1=0.800, Q2=1.28, Q3=40, Q4=50\n- DN65: same as DN50 (R40: 1.250/2.00/40/50; R50: 0.800/1.28/40/50)\n- DN80: R40: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=63, Q4=78.75; R50: Q1=1.260, Q2=2.02, Q3=63, Q4=78.75\n- DN100: R40: Q1=2.500, Q2=4.00, Q3=100, Q4=125; R50: Q1=2.00, Q2=3.20, Q3=100, Q4=125\n- DN150: R40: Q1=6.250, Q2=10.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5; R50: Q1=5.00, Q2=8.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5\n- DN200: R40: Q1=10.00, Q2=16.00, Q3=400, Q4=500; R50: Q1=8.00, Q2=12.8, Q3=400, Q4=500\n\nQ2/Q1 = 1.6 (all), Q4/Q3 = 1.25 (all). Check: DN50 R40: Q1=1.25 → Q3/Q1 = 40/1.25 = 32? Wait that's not 40. Hmm. 40/1.25 = 32. Hmm! Let me check: Q3=40, Q1=1.250 → ratio = 32. But the table says Q3/Q1 = 40 for that column. Hmm, 40/1 = 40. Q1=1.25 gives 32. Something's off. Wait, maybe the columns: Q1 row has 12 values; pairs are (R40, R50) per DN. DN50: R40 → 1.250, R50 → 0.800. Q3=40. 40/1.250 = 32; 40/0.8 = 50. Hmm so R50 works (0.8×50=40 ✓). R40 should be Q1=1.0 for 40/1=40. But cert says 1.250. Whatever — I extract as stated, don't correct. Similarly DN65 same. DN80 R40: 63/1.575 = 40 ✓ (1.575×40 = 63 ✓). R50: 63/1.26 = 50 ✓. DN100 R40: 100/2.5 = 40 ✓; R50: 100/2 = 50 ✓. DN150: 250/6.25 = 40 ✓; 250/5 = 50 ✓. DN200: 400/10 = 40 ✓; 400/8 = 50 ✓. So only DN50/65 R40 values (1.250) are inconsistent with ratio 40 (would be 32), but cert pages 3-4 say Q3/Q1: 40, 50 for LXLC-50. The flowrate table says 1.250. Extract as-is. Not my job to fix.\n\nAlso Q2 values: DN50 R40 Q2=2.00 → Q2/Q1 = 2/1.25 = 1.6 ✓. OK.\n\nNow matrix_tables: I should preserve the \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\" table as a matrix table, and possibly the type-details tables. The schema wants matrix_tables for multi-dim tables. I'll include:\n\n1. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\" — columns: nominal_diameter, ratio variants... The table: rows Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q3/Q1; columns are 12 (6 DN × 2 ratio). Rows as objects: I could pivot: columns: [q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio], rows: one per (DN, ratio) = 12 rows. That's a clean representation. footnotes: none.\n\n2. Maybe also \"Type details\" per design — but that data will be in characteristics/config_level. The instruction: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". The flowrates table fits into model_level too. I'll include it as matrix_table AND populate model_level.\n\nNow, model definition. I'll define models as (DN × ratio × design)? Or (DN × design) with ratio config? Let me count what's cleanest for model_level values.\n\nIf models = 6 DN-based IDs (\"LXLC-50\"...\"LXLC-200\"):\n- model_level: nominal_diameter (values per model), length (per model), min straight lengths (per model), resolution differs by design → config_level axis \"dial_plate_design\" with conditions per model? config_level values have {condition, value} — condition could be \"LXLC-150 (GB)\". Mixing model into condition is messy but workable. Actually config conditions could be \"GB, LXLC-50\" etc.\n\nIf models = 12 (DN × design): \"LXLC-50-GB\", \"LXLC-50-LYG\", etc.:\n- model_level: nominal_diameter, dial_plate_design, indicating_range, resolution, q3 (Q3 same per DN regardless of ratio: yes, Q3=40 for DN50 both ratios, 63 for DN80, etc.), q4, length, min straight lengths.\n- config_level: axis \"q3_q1_ratio\": q1, q2, temperature_class, max admissible temperature.\n\nThat's clean! Q3 and Q4 don't vary by ratio (Q4 = 1.25×Q3). Q1/Q2 vary by ratio. Temperature class varies by ratio.\n\nBut note: LYG only supports R40. If models are DN×design, model \"LXLC-50-LYG\" with config ratio 40 only. I can note in model attributes: {\"available_q3_q1_ratios\": [40, 50]} vs [40]. Good.\n\nSo models: 12 entries:\n- LXLC-50-GB, LXLC-65-GB, LXLC-80-GB, LXLC-100-GB, LXLC-150-GB, LXLC-200-GB\n- LXLC-50-LYG, LXLC-65-LYG, LXLC-80-LYG, LXLC-100-LYG, LXLC-150-LYG, LXLC-200-LYG\n\nAttributes: dial_plate_design: \"GB\"/\"LYG\"; nominal_diameter: 50..200.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. nominal_diameter, unit mm (nominal diameter in mm): 12 values.\n2. dial_plate_design (string): 12 values.\n3. q3, unit m³/h: DN-based: 40,40,63,100,250,400 ×2 designs. 12 values.\n4. q4, unit m³/h: 50,50,78.75,125,312.5,500 ×2.\n5. q3_q1_ratio_available — hmm, maybe attribute \"available_q3_q1_ratios\": GB → \"40; 50\", LYG → \"40\". Could be model attribute instead. I'll put in model attributes.\n6. q2_q1_ratio: 1.6 all (type_level).\n7. q4_q3_ratio: 1.25 all (type_level).\n8. indicating_range, unit m³: GB: 9999999, 9999999, 9999999, 9999999, 99999999, 99999999; LYG: 999999 ×6.\n9. resolution_of_indicating_device, unit m³: GB: 0.002, 0.002, 0.002, 0.002, 0.02, 0.02; LYG: 0.0005×4, 0.002×2.\n10. minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe, mm: 500, 650, 800, 1000, 1250, 1500 ×2.\n11. minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe, mm: 250, 325, 400, 500, 625, 750 ×2.\n12. length, mm: 200, 200, 225, 250, 250, 300 ×2.\n\nconfig_level entries (axis q3_q1_ratio):\n1. attribute q1, unit m³/h: values per (model, condition). With 12 models × conditions... For GB models: condition 40 → q1 R40 value; condition 50 → R50 value. For LYG: condition 40 only. That's 18 value entries.\n   - q1: R40: 1.250, 1.250, 1.575, 2.500, 6.250, 10.00; R50: 0.800, 0.800, 1.260, 2.00, 5.00, 8.00\n2. attribute q2, unit m³/h: R40: 2.00, 2.00, 2.52, 4.00, 10.00, 16.00; R50: 1.28, 1.28, 2.02, 3.20, 8.00, 12.8\n3. attribute temperature_class: condition 40 → \"T30; T50\"; condition 50 → \"T90\".\n4. maximum_admissible_temperature: condition \"T30; T50\" → 50, condition \"T90\" → 90, °C. Hmm axis here is temperature_class, different axis. OK config_level entries can have different axes.\n\nWait, for LYG models, temperature class is T30;T50 only (ratio 40) — consistent.\n\nAlso for GB models the certificate pages 3-4: temperature class per ratio: yes T30;T50 (R40), T90 (R50). Good.\n\nType-level characteristics (whole cert):\n- measuring_principle: \"Woltmann, dry dial\"\n- accuracy_class: \"2\"\n- mpe_lower_zone (maximum_permisible_error_lower_flowrate_zone): ±5 % → value -5 to 5? \"±5 %\" → could be {min: -5, max: 5} with unit \"%\". I'll do value {min:-5, max:5}, unit \"%\".\n- mpe_upper_zone: ±2 % for ≤30°C; ±3 % for >30°C → config_level axis \"water_temperature\": {condition \"≤ 30 °C\", value ±2}, {condition \"> 30 °C\", value ±3}. Value {min:-2,max:2}.\n- water_pressure_class: MAP16\n- pressure_loss_class: Δp63\n- reverse_flow: \"Not designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"H (horizontal position with the indicating device at top)\"\n- connection_type: \"Flange\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U10 D5\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"no\"\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- q4_q3_ratio: 1.25\n- indicating_device variants: dry (LYG Plastic Calculator) or super dry (GB Copper Can Calculator) — components?\n\nComponents: The meters are complete instruments; indicating device variants:\n- role: \"indicating device\", alternatives \"OR\": type_designations [\"LYG (Plastic Calculator, dry)\", \"GB (Copper Can Calculator, super dry)\"].\n- role: \"reed impulse transmitter\" optional remote reading — optional component. Could add: role \"reed impulse transmitter (optional, for remote reading)\".\n\nComponents schema: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll include indicating device with alternatives OR. Maybe also measurement unit? Keep it to indicating device + optional reed transmitter.\n\nFootnotes: none present in the markdown (no footnote definitions). The tables have italic formatting but no numbered footnotes. Skip footnotes.\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2023.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present — omit.\n- page_total: 10\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"31 January 2023\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit. (CMI's issuer id might be \"CM1\"? Not stated. Omit.)\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\"\n- person_title: null — hmm, on page 2 the signatory is \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". The person_responsible on page 1 is Jan Kalandra. Since schema has one person_responsible + person_title, and title belongs to Klenovský... I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and omit title. Or include the signing person somehow? Not in schema. Omit.\n- website: not present. Omit.\n\nTest reports:\n1. {id: \"0511-UL-V037-21\", date: \"2023-01-25\", pages: 31, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — \"31 pages including annex 1\".\n2. {id: \"6015-PT-P5002-23\", pages: 57, role: \"test_report\"} — no date given.\n3. {id: \"0511-UL-V037-21\", role: \"documentation_file\"} — documentation file same number. Include as separate entry with role documentation_file.\n\nDates format: The schema doesn't specify; use \"25 January 2023\" or ISO \"2023-01-25\". I'll use ISO for machine-readability: \"2023-01-25\" and date_issued \"2023-01-31\". Revision history date \"2023-01-31\".\n\nRevision history:\n- revision \"0\" (Addition 0), date \"2023-01-31\", changes \"Issuing certificate\".\n\nMatrix tables:\n1. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\":\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"]\nrows: 12 rows:\n- {nominal_diameter: 50, q3_q1_ratio: 40, q1: 1.250, q2: 2.00, q3: 40, q4: 50}\n- {50, 50, 0.800, 1.28, 40, 50}\n- {65, 40, 1.250, 2.00, 40, 50}\n- {65, 50, 0.800, 1.28, 40, 50}\n- {80, 40, 1.575, 2.52, 63, 78.75}\n- {80, 50, 1.260, 2.02, 63, 78.75}\n- {100, 40, 2.500, 4.00, 100, 125}\n- {100, 50, 2.00, 3.20, 100, 125}\n- {150, 40, 6.250, 10.00, 250, 312.5}\n- {150, 50, 5.00, 8.00, 250, 312.5}\n- {200, 40, 10.00, 16.00, 400, 500}\n- {200, 50, 8.00, 12.8, 400, 500}\n\nNote: values 1.250 → 1.25 numerically; fine.\n\n2. Maybe a \"Type details by model and dial plate design\" matrix table combining resolution etc. — but that data is in model_level. I'll add one combined matrix table \"Type details by dial plate design and nominal diameter\" with columns: dial_plate_design, model, nominal_diameter, temperature_classes, indicating_range, resolution, min_inlet, min_outlet, length. That duplicates model_level but preserves table structure. The instruction says matrix_tables for tables that \"don't fit elsewhere\" — these do fit in model_level. I'll include the flowrates table (most valuable) and maybe one combined GB/LYG details table. I'll include both for fidelity. Hmm, keep it manageable: include the flowrate table and one \"type details\" summary table.\n\nActually, I think I'll include:\n- matrix_tables[0]: Basic metrological data (flowrates)\n- matrix_tables[1]: Type details (GB dial plate) — columns per model...\n- matrix_tables[2]: Type details (LYG dial plate)\n\nHmm, that's 3 tables, heavy. Alternatively just the flowrates one plus rely on model_level for the rest. The GB/LYG type details are fully captured in model_level and config_level. The flowrate table is also captured in model_level+config_level... The rule 4 R137 example emits both matrix_tables AND model_level. I'll follow that pattern: emit the flowrates matrix table + model_level + config_level. And maybe one combined type-details matrix table. Let me include a combined \"Type details\" matrix table with rows per model-design:\n\ncolumns: [\"dial_plate_design\", \"model\", \"nomin