": 0.8},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-80-GB\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 1.575},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-80-GB\", \"condition\": \"50\", \"value\": 1.26},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-100-GB\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 2.5},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-100-GB\", \"condition\": \"50\", \"value\": 2.0},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-150-GB\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 6.25},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-150-GB\", \"condition\": \"50\", \"value\": 5.0},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-200-GB\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 10.0},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-200-GB\", \"condition\": \"50\", \"value\": 8.0},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-50-LYG\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 1.25},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-65-LYG\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 1.25},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-80-LYG\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 1.575},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-100-LYG\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 2.5},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-150-LYG\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 6.25},\n    {\"model\": \"LXLC-200-LYG\", \"condition\": \"40\", \"value\": 10.0}\n  ]\n}\n\nThe config_level schema: {attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}} — the example doesn't include model in values; but rule 5 example \"2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups\". Since I have both model and condition dimensions, including \"model\" in each value entry is necessary. The schema shape says values[]: {condition, value}. I'll add \"model\" key — schema allows? It says values follow the same structured form: {condition, value}. Hmm. Adding \"model\" seems a natural extension and preserves info. Alternatively, treat ratio as part of model (models = 18 combos). Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe cleaner to make models = 18 combos so model_level handles everything and config_level only for temperature (which varies by ratio within model)... but temperature varies by ratio which is then baked into the model. With 18 models, temperature_class becomes model_level too: GB50-40: T30;T50, GB50-50: T90, LYG50: T30;T50. Then q1/q2 model_level. And no config_level needed at all. That's actually cleanest per schema!\n\n18 models: model_id like \"LXLC-50-GB-R40\". Attributes: nominal_diameter, dial_plate_design, q3_q1_ratio.\n\nmodel_level entries then:\n- nominal_diameter: 18 values\n- dial_plate_design: 18 values (string) — or leave in attributes only\n- q3_q1_ratio: 18 values — or attributes only\n- q1: 18 values\n- q2: 18 values\n- q3: 18 values\n- q4: 18 values\n- temperature_class: 18 values (string)\n- indicating_range: 18\n- resolution: 18\n- inlet/outlet/length: 18 each\n\nThat's 12 model_level entries × 18 values = heavy but thorough. Values repeat a lot.\n\nVersus the config approach with model key. Hmm.\n\nThe schema's config_level is designed exactly for axes: \"attribute × config-axis matrix. Values: {condition, value}\". If I choose models = 6 DN-models (per certificate model number!) and design+ratio as config axes, that aligns best with the certificate's own naming: model numbers are LXLC-50..200. Config axes: dial_plate_design (GB/LYG) and q3_q1_ratio (40/50).\n\nThen:\n- model_level: nominal_diameter, length, inlet, outlet (DN-only attributes): 6 values each.\n- config_level:\n  - attribute: resolution_of_indicating_device, axis: dial_plate_design, values: {condition: \"GB, LXLC-50\", value: 0.002}... ugh, conditions need model+design. Condition can be compound: \"GB\", values per model? The config values have {condition, value} — one value per condition. With 6 models × 2 designs, I'd need 12 entries with conditions like \"LXLC-150, dial plate GB\".\n\nOK let me just go with 18-combo models. It's verbose but unambiguous, and each model_level entry is a clean matrix. Actually wait — 12 model_level attributes × 18 values each = 216 value objects. That's a lot of JSON but fine.\n\nAlternatively 12 models (DN×design) with ratio as config axis where config values include model. I think including \"model\" in config values is a minor schema extension but keeps it compact (q1: 18 entries vs 18; same). Hmm, same size actually.\n\n18-model approach: q1 model_level 18 values. 12-model + config: q1 config 18 values (12 GB entries with 2 conditions... wait GB models have 2 ratios: 6×2=12, LYG 6×1=6 → 18 total). Same. And temperature_class: 18-model → model_level 18 values; 12-model → config 18 values (GB c40 ×6, GB c50 ×6, LYG c40 ×6). Same.\n\nDifference: 18-model duplicates DN/design/ratio info in every attribute list? No — DN, design, ratio go in model attributes; model_level attributes don't include them (they're constant per model). With 12-model + config, model_level has DN etc. and design in attributes; config carries ratio-dependent ones.\n\nI'll go with 18 models. IDs: \"LXLC-50-GB-R40\" etc. Hmm, but \"R40\" notation: ratio Q3/Q1=40 commonly written R40 in water meter marking (dial shows \"R50\"). Dial shows \"Q3 40; R50\". So R-notation is authentic. Use model_id \"LXLC-50-GB-R40\".\n\nWait, but then LYG models: \"LXLC-50-LYG-R40\" — a bit long. OK.\n\nAlternatively make model_id = \"LXLC-50 (GB, R40)\". I'll use hyphenated compact.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider size. 18 models × attributes; 12 model_level attributes with 18 values each. Let me list the model_level attributes:\n\n1. nominal_diameter (mm)\n2. q3 (m³/h)\n3. q4 (m³/h)\n4. q1 (m³/h)\n5. q2 (m³/h)\n6. q3_q1_ratio — include? It's in model attributes; but as characteristic it's meaningful. The flowrate table includes Q3/Q1 row. I'll include it: values 40/50.\n7. temperature_class (string)\n8. indicating_range (m³)\n9. resolution_of_indicating_device (m³)\n10. minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe (mm)\n11. minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe (mm)\n12. length (mm)\n\nPlus type_level: q2_q1_ratio 1.6, q4_q3_ratio 1.25 (constant across all).\n\nValues per attribute (order models: 50-GB-R40, 50-GB-R50, 50-LYG-R40, 65-GB-R40, 65-GB-R50, 65-LYG-R40, 80-GB-R40, 80-GB-R50, 80-LYG-R40, 100-GB-R40, 100-GB-R50, 100-LYG-R40, 150-GB-R40, 150-GB-R50, 150-LYG-R40, 200-GB-R40, 200-GB-R50, 200-LYG-R40):\n\nmodel ids: LXLC-50-GB-R40, LXLC-50-GB-R50, LXLC-50-LYG-R40, LXLC-65-GB-R40, LXLC-65-GB-R50, LXLC-65-LYG-R40, LXLC-80-GB-R40, LXLC-80-GB-R50, LXLC-80-LYG-R40, LXLC-100-GB-R40, LXLC-100-GB-R50, LXLC-100-LYG-R40, LXLC-150-GB-R40, LXLC-150-GB-R50, LXLC-150-LYG-R40, LXLC-200-GB-R40, LXLC-200-GB-R50, LXLC-200-LYG-R40.\n\n1. nominal_diameter: 50×3, 65×3, 80×3, 100×3, 150×3, 200×3\n2. q3: 40,40,40 | 40,40,40 | 63,63,63 | 100,100,100 | 250,250,250 | 400,400,400\n3. q4: 50×3, 50×3, 78.75×3, 125×3, 312.5×3, 500×3\n4. q1: 50GB:1.25, 0.8, 1.25(LYG); 65 same; 80: 1.575, 1.26, 1.575; 100: 2.5, 2.0, 2.5; 150: 6.25, 5.0, 6.25; 200: 10.0, 8.0, 10.0\n5. q2: 50: 2.0, 1.28, 2.0; 65 same; 80: 2.52, 2.02, 2.52; 100: 4.0, 3.2, 4.0; 150: 10.0, 8.0, 10.0; 200: 16.0, 12.8, 16.0\n6. q3_q1_ratio: 40,50,40 repeated per DN\n7. temperature_class: \"T30; T50\", \"T90\", \"T30; T50\" per DN (GB R40 → T30/T50; GB R50 → T90; LYG R40 → T30/T50)\n8. indicating_range: GB-50/65/80/100: 9999999; GB-150/200: 99999999; all LYG: 999999\n   → 50: 9999999, 9999999, 999999; 65: 9999999, 9999999, 999999; 80: 9999999, 9999999, 999999; 100: 9999999, 9999999, 999999; 150: 99999999, 99999999, 999999; 200: 99999999, 99999999, 999999\n9. resolution: 50: 0.002, 0.002, 0.0005; 65: same; 80: same; 100: 0.002, 0.002, 0.0005; 150: 0.02, 0.02, 0.002; 200: 0.02, 0.02, 0.002\n10. inlet: 500×3, 650×3, 800×3, 1000×3, 1250×3, 1500×3\n11. outlet: 250×3, 325×3, 400×3, 500×3, 625×3, 750×3\n12. length: 200×3, 200×3, 225×3, 250×3, 250×3, 300×3\n\nGood.\n\nShould I also include dial_plate_design as model_level? It's in model attributes. Skip to reduce bulk.\n\nNow config_level: anything left? The MPE upper zone by water temperature:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\",\n  \"axis\": \"water_temperature\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"> 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nAnd maximum_admissible_temperature by temperature_class? I decided temperature_class values are \"T30; T50\" or \"T90\" as strings; the \"Maximum admissible temperature: 50 or 90\" — map: axis temperature_class: {condition \"T30 or T50\", value 50}, {condition \"T90\", value 90}. I'll include this config entry. It's faithful enough.\n\ntype_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"Woltmann, dry dial\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: {min:-5, max:5} %\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\"\n- reverse_flow: \"Not designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation: \"H\" — value \"H (horizontal, indicating device at top)\"? I'll do value: \"H\", and maybe skip extra. Let me record \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"H\", ...} plus \"installation_position\": \"horizontal only with indicating device at top\"? Hmm. The cert has \"Orientation limitation: H (for horizontal position with the indicating device at top)\". I'll record orientation_limitation: {\"value\": \"H (horizontal position with the indicating device at top)\"} — string value including parenthetical, that's fine as a string. Actually cleaner: value \"H\". But losing info. Keep the parenthetical in the string; it's allowed (string values allowed).\n\nHmm, but canonical labels should be clean, values can be strings. OK.\n\n- connection_type: \"Flange\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U10 D5\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"no\"\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- q4_q3_ratio: 1.25\n- installation_position? redundant with orientation. skip.\n\nStructured value objects for type_level:\n\"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Woltmann, dry dial\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n\"water_pressure_class\": {\"value\": \"MAP16\", ...}\n\"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"Δp63\", ...}\n\"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"Not designed to measure\", ...}\n\"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"O\", ...}\n\"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"}\n\"orientation_limitation\": {\"value\": \"H\", ...} — I'll include description in separate? Let me just put value \"H\". Hmm, actually let me put value: \"H (horizontal position with the indicating device at top)\" — keeps all info in one field. OK.\n\"connection_type\": {\"value\": \"Flange\", ...}\n\"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U10 D5\", ...}\n\"flow_conditioner\": {\"value\": \"no\", ...}\n\"q2_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, ...}\n\"q4_q3_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.25, ...}\n\nmatrix_tables:\n\nTable 1: \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\"\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"]\nrows: 12 rows as above.\n\nTable 2: \"Type details by model and dial plate design\"\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"dial_plate_design\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"temperature_class\", \"indicating_range\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\", \"min_straight_length_inlet_pipe\", \"min_straight_length_outlet_pipe\", \"length\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"mm\", null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\"]\nrows: 18 rows.\n\nHmm — including both matrix_tables and full model_level is very redundant. The R137 example did include both. But given size constraints, maybe include Table 1 (the explicitly named \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\" table) and skip Table 2 since model_level fully captures it... The instruction: matrix_tables for tables that \"don't fit elsewhere\". Table 2's data fits in model_level. Table 1 also fits (q1..q4 in model_level). Honestly the whole thing fits in model_level. But R137 example emitted both anyway. I'll include both tables — fidelity to source tables. Actually, let me include both; JSON size is fine.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider — 18-row table 2 plus 12-row table 1 plus model_level 12×18... it's a big JSON but that's fine. It's mechanical.\n\nActually, I'll trim: include Table 1 (flowrates) as matrix_table. For the type details, the model_level covers it. But I'll add Table 2 anyway for fidelity... Let me think about what a consumer wants: normalized model_level + matrix tables for verification. I'll include both. OK final: include both.\n\nComponents:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"indicating device\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"LYG – Plastic Calculator (dry)\", \"GB – Copper Can Calculator (super dry)\"],\n    \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n    \"characteristics\": {}\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"reed impulse transmitter (optional, for remote reading)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {}\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, \"characteristics\" for indicating device: number_of_drums variants: six drums/three pointers or seven drums/two pointers. Add to characteristics? characteristics: {\"register_configurations\": [\"six drums and three rotary pointers\", \"seven drums and two rotary pointers\"]}? Free-form ok. I'll add briefly.\n\nMaybe skip empty characteristics. Schema allows characteristics{}; I'll include only where meaningful.\n\nTest reports:\n\n[\n  {\"id\": \"0511-UL-V037-21\", \"date\": \"2023-01-25\", \"pages\": 31, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"6015-PT-P5002-23\", \"pages\": 57, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"0511-UL-V037-21\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n\nRevision history:\n\n[{\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2023-01-31\", \"changes\": \"Issuing certificate (Addition 0)\"}]\n\nrevision string \"0\".\n\nRecommendation:\n\n{\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [2]}\n\nCertificate:\n\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2023.01\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"page_total\": 10,\n  \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2023-01-31\"\n}\n\nOmit project_number, oiml_issuer_id (not present).\n\nCertified type:\n\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"LXLC\", \"LXSG-20\"],\n  \"module_designation\": \"Not applicable\",\n  \"description\": \"Horizontal Woltmann water meter consisting of a cast iron body with connecting flanges, an interchangeable wet measurement unit with adjusting device and a dry mechanical indicating device or super dry mechanical indicating device (Copper Can Calculator). Optionally equipped with a reed impulse transmitter for remote reading. Indicating devices: numbered rollers with six drums and three rotary pointers, or seven drums and two rotary pointers. Horizontal installation only, indicating device positioned at the top.\"\n}\n\nWait, about \"LXSG-20\" — hmm. Let me reconsider once more. The marking section: \"The water meters types LXSG-20 shall be clearly and indelibly marked...\" This is almost certainly an error in the certificate (copy-paste from another cert; LXSG is another Cixi Cidong product line). Including it in type_designations could mislead a user into thinking LXSG-20 is certified. But the extractor's job is extraction, not correction. Rule: \"type_designations[] — all type/model identifiers mentioned\". I'll include it. Someone verifying against source will find it. OK include.\n\nAlso should \"Woltmann\" be in type_designations? No, that's the kind.\n\nFootnotes: none. Omit footnotes[].\n\nNow, is \"date_issued\" definitely 31 January 2023? Page 2: \"Date: 31 January 2023\" with signature. Revision history Addition 0 same date. Yes.\n\nIssuing authority person_title: On page 1 \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\" — no title. Page 2 signature: \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". I could add \"person_title\": \"Head of Certification Body\" — but that maps to Klenovský, not Kalandra. Mixing would be wrong. I'll omit person_title. Alternatively include a second note? Schema is flat; omit. Hmm — or set person_responsible to Klenovský? No — page 1 explicitly says person responsible is Jan Kalandra. Keep Kalandra.\n\nLet me also double check \"oiml_issuer_id\" — not present anywhere. Omit.\n\nNow assemble the full JSON. Watch out for special characters: \"Δp63\", \"≤\", \"m³/h\", \"Okružní\", \"Kalen...\" — all fine in JSON as UTF-8.\n\nLet me write out model entries for model_family.models:\n\n1. {\"model_id\": \"LXLC-50-GB-R40\", \"label\": \"LXLC – 50, dial plate GB, Q3/Q1 = 40\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"dial_plate_design\": \"GB\", \"calculator_type\": \"Copper Can Calculator (super dry)\", \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40}}\n2. {\"model_id\": \"LXLC-50-GB-R50\", \"label\": \"LXLC – 50, dial plate GB, Q3/Q1 = 50\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"dial_plate_design\": \"GB\", \"calculator_type\": \"Copper Can Calculator (super dry)\", \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50}}\n3. {\"model_id\": \"LXLC-50-LYG-R40\", \"label\": \"LXLC – 50, dial plate LYG, Q3/Q1 = 40\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"dial_plate_design\": \"LYG\", \"calculator_type\": \"Plastic Calculator (dry)\", \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40}}\n... etc for 65, 80, 100, 150, 200.\n\nfamily_name: \"LXLC\".\n\nNow model_level values arrays. Model order (I'll use consistent order): 50-GB-R40, 50-GB-R50, 50-LYG-R40, 65-GB-R40, 65-GB-R50, 65-LYG-R40, 80-GB-R40, 80-GB-R50, 80-LYG-R40, 100-GB-R40, 100-GB-R50, 100-LYG-R40, 150-GB-R40, 150-GB-R50, 150-LYG-R40, 200-GB-R40, 200-GB-R50, 200-LYG-R40.\n\nTo keep value objects compact, omit unit_symbol inside individual values when the attribute entry has a unit? The schema model_level example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]} — unit at attribute level, values only {model, value}. Good, follow that.\n\nmatrix_tables rows — include model column referencing composite id? In table 2 rows: {\"model\": \"LXLC-50-GB-R40\", \"dial_plate_design\": \"GB\", ...}. Fine.\n\nTable 1 rows: {\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 1.25, \"q2\": 2.0, \"q3\": 40, \"q4\": 50}, etc.\n\nAdd description to matrix tables: table 1: \"Flowrates for water meters type LXLC by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio (applies to both dial plate designs; dial plate LYG is available with Q3/Q1 = 40 only).\" footnotes: [].\n\nWait — does the flowrate table apply to LYG? The GB type-details tables say \"flowrates are shown in Table Basic metrological data (flowrates)\". The LYG type-details table also says that. And the flowrate table itself says model LXLC without design restriction. So yes applies to both; LYG only uses R40 columns. Good.\n\nNow config_level:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\",\n    \"axis\": \"water_temperature\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"water temperature > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_admissible_temperature\",\n    \"axis\": \"temperature_class\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"T30 or T50\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"T90\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nHmm — is the second config faithful? Cert: \"Maximum admissible temperature [°C]: 50 or 90\" alongside temperature class per variant. The mapping is natural. Keep it.\n\nAlso could add mpe_lower as config? No, it's constant ±5%.\n\nOne more check: the GB table on page 5 (models 100/150/200) — indicating range row is on page 6 continuation with 3 columns (colspan differences): 9 999 999, 99 999 999, 99 999 999. And resolution 0.002, 0.02, 0.02. Yes.\n\nLet me also confirm LYG resolution column mapping: \"Resolution of the indicating device [m³]: 0.0005, 0.0005, 0.0005, 0.0005, 0.002, 0.002\" for models 50,65,80,100,150,200. Yes.\n\nLYG indicating range: \"999 999\" ×6 = 999999. Yes.\n\nNow double-check GB-100 indicating range: page 6 \"Indicating range [m³]: 9 999 999 | 99 999 999 | 99 999 999\" for 100/150/200 → GB-100: 9999999, GB-150: 99999999, GB-200: 99999999. Yes.\n\nNow the \"matrix\" attribute names: use q1, q2, q3, q4 canonical snake labels; subscript handling: Q3 → q3.\n\nWrite the JSON now. Also include \"certificate.project_number\"? Not present — omit.\n\nOne thing: scheme field — \"A\". Yes, \"OIML CERTIFICATE ISSUED UNDER SCHEME A\".\n\nAlso certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2023.01\".\n\nTest report pages: \"31 pages including annex 1\" → pages: 31. \"57 pages including annex 1, 2, 3\" → pages: 57.\n\nTest report 1 date \"25 January 2023\" → \"2023-01-25\".\n\nNow the components characteristics for indicating device: The register variants: \"Numbered rollers with six drums and three rotary pointers\" or \"Numbered rollers with seven drums and two rotary pointers\". Add:\n\"characteristics\": {\"register_variants\": [\"numbered rollers with six drums and three rotary pointers\", \"numbered rollers with seven drums and two rotary pointers\"]}\n\nOK. Also reed transmitter: optional component. role: \"reed impulse transmitter (optional, for remote reading)\".\n\nLet me also consider adding a component for the \"measurement unit\" (interchangeable wet measurement unit with adjusting device). The cert describes it as part of construction, not a separately certified module. Module designation: Not applicable. Skip.\n\nFinal JSON assembly. Ensure valid JSON: no trailing commas, proper escaping. Use \"–\" en-dashes as in source for labels — fine.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nAlso decide: \"characteristics\" top-level keys: \"type_level\", \"model_level\", \"config_level\". Values in model_level: {model, value}. Should value objects include unit_symbol? No — attribute-level unit.\n\nFor model_level string attributes (temperature_class), attribute entry: {\"attribute\": \"temperature_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [...]}? The example included unit_symbol for numeric. For temperature_class I'll include \"unit_symbol\": null or omit. I'll omit unit_symbol for string attributes to reduce noise... but consistency helps. I'll include \"unit_symbol\": null only where relevant. Eh, I'll include unit_symbol for numeric attributes and omit for string ones.\n\nActually for cleanliness, include \"unit_symbol\" on all model_level attribute entries (null for strings). Fine.\n\nNow count model_level entries: 12. Let me write them carefully.\n\nModel order shorthand: m1=LXLC-50-GB-R40, m2=LXLC-50-GB-R50, m3=LXLC-50-LYG-R40, m4=LXLC-65-GB-R40, m5=LXLC-65-GB-R50, m6=LXLC-65-LYG-R40, m7=LXLC-80-GB-R40, m8=LXLC-80-GB-R50, m9=LXLC-80-LYG-R40, m10=LXLC-100-GB-R40, m11=LXLC-100-GB-R50, m12=LXLC-100-LYG-R40, m13=LXLC-150-GB-R40, m14=LXLC-150-GB-R50, m15=LXLC-150-LYG-R40, m16=LXLC-200-GB-R40, m17=LXLC-200-GB-R50, m18=LXLC-200-LYG-R40.\n\n1. nominal_diameter (mm): m1:50 m2:50 m3:50 m4:65 m5:65 m6:65 m7:80 m8:80 m9:80 m10:100 m11:100 m12:100 m13:150 m14:150 m15:150 m16:200 m17:200 m18:200\n\n2. q3 (m³/h): m1:40 m2:40 m3:40 m4:40 m5:40 m6:40 m7:63 m8:63 m9:63 m10:100 m11:100 m12:100 m13:250 m14:250 m15:250 m16:400 m17:400 m18:400\n\n3. q4 (m³/h): 50×6, 78.75×3, 125×3, 312.5×3, 500×3\n\n4. q1 (m³/h): m1:1.25 m2:0.8 m3:1.25 m4:1.25 m5:0.8 m6:1.25 m7:1.575 m8:1.26 m9:1.575 m10:2.5 m11:2.0 m12:2.5 m13:6.25 m14:5.0 m15:6.25 m16:10.0 m17:8.0 m18:10.0\n\n5. q2 (m³/h): m1:2.0 m2:1.28 m3:2.0 m4:2.0 m5:1.28 m6:2.0 m7:2.52 m8:2.02 m9:2.52 m10:4.0 m11:3.2 m12:4.0 m13:10.0 m14:8.0 m15:10.0 m16:16.0 m17:12.8 m18:16.0\n\n6. q3_q1_ratio: 40,50,40,40,50,40,40,50,40,40,50,40,40,50,40,40,50,40\n\n7. temperature_class: \"T30; T50\",\"T90\",\"T30; T50\" ×6 (per DN block)\n\n8. indicating_range (m³): m1:9999999 m2:9999999 m3:999999 m4:9999999 m5:9999999 m6:999999 m7:9999999 m8:9999999 m9:999999 m10:9999999 m11:9999999 m12:999999 m13:99999999 m14:99999999 m15:999999 m16:99999999 m17:99999999 m18:999999\n\n9. resolution_of_indicating_device (m³): m1:0.002 m2:0.002 m3:0.0005 m4:0.002 m5:0.002 m6:0.0005 m7:0.002 m8:0.002 m9:0.0005 m10:0.002 m11:0.002 m12:0.0005 m13:0.02 m14:0.02 m15:0.002 m16:0.02 m17:0.02 m18:0.002\n\n10. min straight inlet (mm): 500,500,500,650,650,650,800,800,800,1000,1000,1000,1250,1250,1250,1500,1500,1500\n\n11. min straight outlet (mm): 250,250,250,325,325,325,400,400,400,500,500,500,625,625,625,750,750,750\n\n12. length (mm): 200,200,200,200,200,200,225,225,225,250,250,250,250,250,250,300,300,300\n\nWait length: 50:200, 65:200, 80:225, 100:250, 150:250, 200:300. So per block: 200×3, 200×3, 225×3, 250×3, 250×3, 300×3. Yes as written.\n\nMatrix table 2 rows: 18 rows with all columns. Let me generate.\n\nRow format: {\"model\": \"LXLC-50-GB-R40\", \"dial_plate_design\": \"GB\", \"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"temperature_class\": \"T30; T50\", \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"resolution_of_indicating_device\": 0.002, \"min_straight_length_inlet_pipe\": 500, \"min_straight_length_outlet_pipe\": 250, \"length\": 200}\n\nHmm should I include q1/q2/q3/q4 in table 2 as well? That'd duplicate table 1. Keep table 2 to type details; table 1 to flowrates. Good.\n\nTable 1 rows:\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 1.25, \"q2\": 2.0, \"q3\": 40, \"q4\": 50}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 0.8, \"q2\": 1.28, \"q3\": 40, \"q4\": 50}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 65, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 1.25, \"q2\": 2.0, \"q3\": 40, \"q4\": 50}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 65, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 0.8, \"q2\": 1.28, \"q3\": 40, \"q4\": 50}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 80, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 1.575, \"q2\": 2.52, \"q3\": 63, \"q4\": 78.75}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 80, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 1.26, \"q2\": 2.02, \"q3\": 63, \"q4\": 78.75}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 100, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 2.5, \"q2\": 4.0, \"q3\": 100, \"q4\": 125}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 100, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 2.0, \"q2\": 3.2, \"q3\": 100, \"q4\": 125}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 150, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 6.25, \"q2\": 10.0, \"q3\": 250, \"q4\": 312.5}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 150, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 5.0, \"q2\": 8.0, \"q3\": 250, \"q4\": 312.5}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 200, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 10.0, \"q2\": 16.0, \"q3\": 400, \"q4\": 500}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 200, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 8.0, \"q2\": 12.8, \"q3\": 400, \"q4\": 500}\n\nOK. Now write final JSON. I'll be careful with JSON validity.\n\nAlso: should characteristics.type_level include \"maximum_admissible_temperature\"? I put it in config_level. Good.\n\nOne more consideration: certificate \"scheme\": \"A\". Good.\n\nWriting final answer now.",
